XDJ-RX patch 21: sub-millisecond main-loop timers on macOS (no ppoll).

QEMU's qemu_poll_ns() falls back to g_poll() with the timeout rounded UP to whole
milliseconds when ppoll is unavailable (macOS), so every sub-ms timer fired ~1 ms
late. That is what kept EHCI microframe polling (patch 20) from helping: each USB
transfer stage still cost ~1.5 ms. Now a timeout with a sub-ms part polls for the
whole milliseconds, then nanosleeps the remainder and polls once more without
blocking; an fd event during the remainder is noticed < 1 ms late (no worse than
before). Linux hosts (ppoll) are unaffected. Guest audio and host CPU unchanged.

--- a/util/qemu-timer.c
+++ b/util/qemu-timer.c
@@ -343,6 +343,23 @@
         return ppoll((struct pollfd *)fds, nfds, &ts, NULL);
     }
 #else
+    /*
+     * No ppoll (macOS): poll() only takes whole milliseconds, and rounding the
+     * timeout up to the next ms made every sub-ms timer fire ~1 ms late (e.g. the
+     * EHCI controller's microframe polling: each USB transfer stage cost ~1.5 ms,
+     * so mass-storage reads crawled at ~1.7 MB/s). Poll for the whole ms, then
+     * sleep the sub-ms remainder and poll once more without blocking. An fd event
+     * during that remainder is noticed < 1 ms late -- no worse than the rounding.
+     */
+    if (timeout > 0 && (timeout % SCALE_MS) != 0) {
+        int ret = g_poll(fds, nfds, (gint)MIN(timeout / SCALE_MS, INT32_MAX));
+        if (ret != 0) {
+            return ret;
+        }
+        struct timespec ts = { 0, timeout % SCALE_MS };
+        nanosleep(&ts, NULL);
+        return g_poll(fds, nfds, 0);
+    }
     return g_poll(fds, nfds, qemu_timeout_ns_to_ms(timeout));
 #endif
 }
